 "decisions": [
  {
   "title": "Data migrates to the target database",
   "context": "The target keeps its data in SQL Server, not on the legacy engine. The payment reads and updates pg_orden, ad_tarifa_empresa, ad_servicio and ct_cuenta, and calls db_cuentas..sp_debito, which owns the account movements.",
   "decision": "Use data_strategy migration: the four tables move to the target database with snake_case table names and the legacy column names, and the repositories work on them. The debit is behind the DebitGateway port; its target implementation reproduces the contract of sp_debito (movement sequence and return status) on the migrated account data, within the same transaction as the use case.",
   "consequences": "A data migration of the four tables must be run and reconciled before cut-over. The account movement logic of sp_debito has to be provided in the target and verified with the golden master. The legacy qualified names (db_pagos.., db_admin.., db_cuentas..) disappear from the target."
  },
  {
   "title": "Transaction boundary of PayOrder",
   "context": "The legacy opens the transaction at line 108, after all validations, and commits at line 161. It rolls back at lines 119, 136 and 155.",
   "decision": "The use case opens the transaction just before the first debit and commits after the order update. Any failure of the first debit (non-zero return, or an exception in place of @@error), the commission debit or the order update (error or zero rows) rolls back everything and rejects with DEBIT_FAILED, COMMISSION_FAILED or ORDER_UPDATE_FAILED. There are no savepoints, so no UnitOfWork port is needed.",
   "consequences": "Validation errors 50001 to 50004 happen before the transaction and need no rollback. A commission failure undoes the already successful main debit."
  },
  {
   "title": "Computations kept as in the legacy",
   "context": "Tariff, commission and total follow rules RULE-004 to RULE-006 with money precision decimal(19,4).",
   "decision": "Tariff comes from the company row (tar_valor and tar_separada); with no company row it comes from the service (srv_tarifa) with flag 'N'. A null tariff becomes 0. The WEB channel pays round(tariff/2, 2) and any other channel pays the full tariff. NOMINA always has commission 0. The total is the amount when the flag is 'S', otherwise amount plus commission. If the company row exists but its tar_valor is null, there is no fallback to the service tariff and the tariff is 0. The default channel 'WEB' of the legacy parameter is applied when the caller sends no channel.",
   "consequences": "The rounding mode of round(x/2, 2) is verified in the golden master replay. The default channel is a contract detail of the API."
  },
  {
   "title": "Missing account does not reject the payment by itself",
   "context": "In the legacy, if the account lookup returns no row, @w_saldo stays null and the comparisons with null are not true, so error 50004 is not raised.",
   "decision": "When AccountRepository returns no account, the balance is treated as unknown and the funds check does not reject. The flow goes on to the debit, where the debit decides.",
   "consequences": "Behaviour is identical to the legacy. A reviewer may want to turn this into an explicit rejection in a later change."
  },
  {
   "title": "Error plumbing is not translated",
   "context": "The legacy sets @w_error, jumps to ERROR and calls cobis..sp_cerror, then returns the error code.",
   "decision": "Business errors are exceptions with a stable code and the legacy message, mapped to the HTTP response. sp_cerror is infrastructure and is not called. DebitResult is a non-persistent result entity that carries the movement and the return status of the debit.",
   "consequences": "The error log of cobis is not written by the target unless the platform provides it."
  }
